# WasmBox API reference

All tenant-scoped endpoints require an Authorization header containing Bearer followed by the signed demo token returned from the demo-login endpoint.

## Login

POST /api/auth/demo-login

~~~json
{ "tenant_slug": "acme-demo" }
~~~

Response:

~~~json
{ "access_token": "signed-token", "token_type": "bearer" }
~~~

## Create and compile a plugin

POST /api/plugins

~~~json
{
  "name": "Invoice formatter",
  "source": "def transform(input):\n    return {\"total\": input[\"amount\"] * 1.18}\n"
}
~~~

The create response includes a webhook secret once. Normal GET and list responses deliberately do not return it.

POST /api/plugins/{plugin_id}/compile validates source, lowers it to WAT/Wasm, validates imports and memory declaration, and persists the artifact.

## Run a plugin

POST /api/plugins/{plugin_id}/run

~~~json
{ "input": { "amount": 1000 } }
~~~

Success response:

~~~json
{
  "id": "run-id",
  "status": "succeeded",
  "stdout": "Invoice formatting started",
  "output": { "total": 1180.0 },
  "error": null,
  "metrics": {
    "execution_ms": 0.84,
    "fuel_consumed": 142,
    "linear_memory_bytes": 65536,
    "memory_limit_bytes": 10485760
  },
  "trigger": "manual"
}
~~~

Metrics values are examples; never copy them into a report as a benchmark result.

## Simulated webhook

POST /api/plugins/{plugin_id}/trigger has the same input body as /run and records trigger as simulated-webhook. It exists so the portal can demonstrate event-driven execution without exposing a real internet endpoint.

## Signed webhook

POST /api/webhooks/{plugin_id}

Headers:

~~~text
Content-Type: application/json
X-WasmBox-Timestamp: unix-seconds
X-WasmBox-Signature: sha256=<HMAC-SHA256>
~~~

The HMAC is calculated with the one-time webhook secret over this byte sequence:

~~~text
ASCII(timestamp) + "." + raw HTTP request body
~~~

Requests older than five minutes, invalid IDs, missing headers, and invalid signatures are rejected without revealing tenant information.

## Error format

Expected errors have a stable, safe response shape:

~~~json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "filesystem_capability_unavailable",
    "message": "open is denied because the Wasm sandbox has no filesystem capability."
  }
}
~~~

Useful error codes include plugin_not_compiled, filesystem_capability_unavailable, network_capability_unavailable, loops_forbidden, forbidden_import, memory_limit_violation, execution_timeout, fuel_exhausted, and not_found.
